{
	title: 'Colours as code',
	spec: "src/common/colors.ts is the theme (each colour is fitted to sRGB, and the terminal and the web page get the same fitted colour, the page as CSS oklch(L C h) so DevTools can tweak it: they must look alike): one exported object whose top-level fields are read at call time, holding every colour Hal draws, terminal and web: nothing is computed outside it, so a theme can change any colour. Derivations are fields too (quiet(fg, bg): secondary text; blinkDim(fg): the dark phase of a blinking tab mark; input.placeholder: the example request; mix: the colour-mix percentages for hover, the current tab, the prompt bar, quote and table lines, the divider, the tab sheet backdrop and the busy pulse, emitted as --mix-* on :root). Otherwise: shared values, plain (fgL: 0.8, fgC, bgL, bgC, screen, one colour, and mix), and styles, functions computed from them (assistant: () => ({ fg: [colors.fgL, colors.fgC, 55] })). local.ts overrides a value (colors.fgL = 0.9) or a style (colors.user = () => ...), and a plugin replaces a value with plugin.set(colors, 'fgL', 0.9) and a function with around(colors, 'user', ...) (task d41), both undone with the plugin; everything derived follows. Styles stay functions even when constant: themes extend them through the field they replace. No string references like $fgL and no colors.ason. The terminal turns styles into escape codes; the host generates CSS from the same object for the web page, which never sees the functions. Hal is dark only: there is no light theme and no light/dark setting (the user's decision). Reference for the palette: the old colors.ason and colors.ts (artifacts); keep its look, not its format. Every colour obeys the readable-text invariant of tasks/README.md: colors.screen is the lightest dark background designed for, and a test measures every text part of every style against its own bg (else screen), and against linkBg, at 4.5:1 (3:1 for cursor marks; page.border 3:1 on the canvas). Secondary text (tool glimpses, the `$ command` beside a description, block ids, hints, table rules, code fences, quote bars, in-flight markers, inbox tags) is colors.quiet(fg, bg), by default oklch.quiet: a lightness step darker, never under 4.6:1, drawn by ansi.quiet, which returns to the style fg after. The terminal never uses SGR 2 (faint); the web never fades resting text by opacity: host/web.ts emits --quiet per style (the page's measured on its button surface) and the CSS uses it.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Every style field of colors is a function of no arguments returning { part: [L, C, H] }; shared values (fgL, fgC, bgL, bgC, screen, mix) are plain (task d41): host/web.ts css() skips non-functions (mix is emitted as --mix-*), calls each style, and for styles makes class .kebab-key (toolBash -> .tool-bash), fg -> color, bg -> background-color, other parts -> custom properties (--link-bg). Put helpers elsewhere (frame.toolStyle).',
		'Per-tool colours are fields tool + Capitalised name (toolBash); aliases are functions calling another (toolGrep: () => colors.toolRead()). Unknown tools fall back to colors.tool; web view emits "tool tool-<name>" so CSS does the same fallback.',
		'CSS is substituted into the cached page on every request, so eval/local.ts overrides show on reload. src/web never imports colors.ts.',
		'Out-of-gamut OKLCH keeps hue and chroma and darkens until it fits (common/oklch.ts fit), down to the most vivid point of the hue at most; only past that, or for a colour already darker (a card background), does it lose chroma. Never per-channel clipping, and never keep lightness by losing chroma for a bright colour: that turned the bright orange of Hal into pale skin-pink peach in the terminal, while the web (unfitted, wide gamut) showed orange. The user wants vivid, like the old Hal.',
		'Rows with a bg are painted as full-width cards (frame.paint); every styled row ends with SGR 39;49, so tests strip SGR (strings.walk) before comparing text.'
